0. Getting the packages
-----------------------

Release builds of both packages are attached to every CARLOS GitHub release
(https://github.com/carlos-emr/carlos/releases) by the "Debian Packages"
workflow, alongside their .sha256 checksums and build-provenance
attestations. The carlos-emr package on a release ships that release's own
published WAR byte for byte. Verify, then install:

  sha256sum -c carlos-emr_<version>_all.deb.sha256
  sha256sum -c carlos-emr-drugref_<version>_all.deb.sha256
  sudo apt install ./carlos-emr_<version>_all.deb ./carlos-emr-drugref_<version>_all.deb

Building from source instead: see debian/rules (the header documents the
inputs, including how to skip the Maven compile with a prebuilt WAR).


1. What gets installed and where
--------------------------------

  /usr/share/carlos-emr/webapp/carlos/   the exploded application, root-owned
                                         and read-only to the service account
  /usr/share/carlos-emr/webapp/drugref2/ DrugRef2 (carlos-emr-drugref)
  /usr/share/carlos-emr/schema/          the Flyway migration set, readable
  /usr/lib/carlos-emr/                   the administrative helpers
  /usr/sbin/carlos-ctl                    the administration command

  /etc/carlos-emr/carlos-emr.env         site settings; yours to edit
  /etc/carlos-emr/carlos.properties      application configuration + credentials
  /etc/carlos-emr/drugref2.properties    DrugRef configuration + credentials
  /etc/carlos-emr/backup.env             backup repository + credentials
  /etc/carlos-emr/tomcat/                Tomcat configuration (conffiles)
  /etc/carlos-emr/modsecurity/           WAF policy and exclusions (conffiles)
  /etc/carlos-emr/tls/                   the served certificate and key
  /etc/nginx/sites-available/carlos-emr  the front door (conffile)
  /etc/mysql/mariadb.conf.d/60-carlos-emr.cnf   MariaDB settings (conffile)

  /var/lib/carlos-emr/OscarDocument/     PATIENT DOCUMENTS
  /var/lib/carlos-emr/catalina/          Tomcat's writable state
  /var/lib/carlos-emr/heapdumps/         JVM heap dumps (PHI — see below)
  /var/log/carlos-emr/tomcat/            application, container and access logs
  /var/log/carlos-emr/modsec/            ModSecurity audit and debug logs
  /var/backups/carlos-emr/               the local restic repository

Both /var/lib/carlos-emr and /var/log/carlos-emr are root-owned containers;
every subdirectory is owned by the account that writes it. That is not
cosmetic: systemd-tmpfiles refuses to descend from a directory owned by one
unprivileged user into one owned by another, so a service-owned parent would
leave the nginx-owned subdirectories silently uncreated.

A heap dump written by -XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError contains whatever
clinical data was in memory when the JVM ran out. It lands in
/var/lib/carlos-emr/heapdumps (0750 carlos:carlos), never /tmp. Treat one as
you would a database dump.

Day-two administration is one loop: edit the file, run the verb beside it.

  carlos-emr.env      -> sudo carlos-ctl init-config   (re-renders the nginx
                         fragments, refreshes a stale self-signed certificate,
                         config-tests and reloads nginx, and says whether an
                         application restart is also needed)
  carlos.properties   -> sudo carlos-ctl restart
  backup.env          -> nothing; the next timer run reads it. Prove it early
                         with: sudo carlos-ctl backup full
  modsecurity/*       -> sudo carlos-ctl waf reload    (config-tested first; a
                         typo cannot take the front door down)
  tomcat/*            -> sudo carlos-ctl restart

`carlos-ctl restart` is a passthrough to systemd; expect about two minutes for
the webapp to redeploy. `sudo carlos-ctl check` after any change confirms the
whole system end to end.

Which files are conffiles matters. Anything listed above as a conffile is
yours to edit and dpkg will offer a three-way merge on upgrade. The four files
carrying credentials are NOT conffiles: the package writes them once and never
overwrites them, so an upgrade cannot invalidate a working credential. After an
upgrade, compare them against the current reference copies in
/usr/share/carlos-emr/skel/ to see whether new keys are available.


2. What runs as whom
--------------------

  carlos          the JVM serving the EMR. Owns patient documents and holds
                  the read/write database credential.
  carlos-backup   the backup and restore-drill timers. Member of group
                  `carlos` so it can READ application data; the application is
                  in no group of its own, so it cannot read the backup
                  repository, its passphrase, or the backup credential. A
                  compromise of the EMR therefore cannot destroy or decrypt
                  the backups you would recover from.
  www-data        nginx, the only process listening on a public interface.
  mysql           MariaDB, listening on loopback only.

No long-running component runs as root. Root is used by the package maintainer
scripts, and by the twice-daily certificate-renewal timer, which touches
/etc/letsencrypt and reloads nginx and nothing else.

nginx's master process starts as root in order to bind ports 80 and 443 and
immediately drops to www-data for every worker. That is the distribution's own
design and this package does not change it.

Verify all of this on a running system with:  sudo carlos-ctl check


3. First run
------------

  sudo carlos-ctl check

runs the full deployment check: services, process ownership, what is listening
where, certificate validity, the security headers, whether the WAF actually
blocks a probe attack, schema state, and backup freshness. Start there.

The database migrations seed one administrator account, `carlosdoc`, whose
password hash is published in the CARLOS source repository. The package
replaces that password and PIN with random values at install time and writes
them to /etc/carlos-emr/initial-admin.txt (mode 0600, root only).

That account arrives flagged for a FORCED PASSWORD RESET — the state the
migrations seed it in, which this package deliberately keeps. Your first login
goes straight to the reset page: it asks for the generated password as the
"old" one and a new password of your choosing. That matters, because the
generated password exists in a file on disk and must not become the long-term
credential.

The PIN is four digits, and that is not a package choice: Login2Action
discards any submitted PIN that does not match [0-9]{4} and then compares the
resulting empty string, so a longer PIN fails authentication with a message
saying the *password* was wrong. If you set PINs by hand, use four digits.

Then create real named accounts for each clinician, disable `carlosdoc`, and
delete /etc/carlos-emr/initial-admin.txt.


4. TLS certificates
-------------------

nginx always reads /etc/carlos-emr/tls/fullchain.pem and privkey.pem. What is
behind those two paths is managed by carlos-ctl, so switching certificate
providers never involves editing nginx configuration.

  sudo carlos-ctl cert status          what is being served now
  sudo carlos-ctl cert selfsigned      regenerate the self-signed pair
  sudo carlos-ctl cert acme you@clinic.ca
                                      switch to Let's Encrypt
  sudo carlos-ctl cert manual          adopt files you placed yourself

The default is self-signed, on purpose: it means the EMR is served over TLS
from the first minute rather than sitting on plain HTTP "until we get the
certificate sorted out". Browsers will warn until you replace it.

`carlos-ctl cert acme` needs the host name in /etc/carlos-emr/carlos-emr.env to
resolve in public DNS to this machine, and port 80 reachable from the internet
for the HTTP-01 challenge. It uses certbot's webroot method rather than the
nginx plugin, because the plugin rewrites the site file — a dpkg conffile —
behind your back.

The carlos-emr-cert-renew.timer runs twice a day. In acme mode it renews; in
selfsigned mode it re-issues a certificate that is within 30 days of expiry;
in manual mode it FAILS THE UNIT if your certificate expires within 21 days,
so that a front door about to go dark is loud rather than silent.


5. The web application firewall
-------------------------------

nginx runs ModSecurity 3 with the OWASP Core Rule Set at paranoia level 1, in
BLOCKING mode, with the CRS default anomaly thresholds (inbound 5, outbound
4). Those thresholds are not arbitrary: a single critical-severity signature
scores 5, so one clean SQL-injection, cross-site-scripting or
remote-code-execution hit blocks. The widely-copied 10/5 pair would log such an
attack and let it through.

  sudo carlos-ctl waf status           engine state and file locations
  sudo carlos-ctl waf tail 200         recent blocks, in readable form
  sudo carlos-ctl waf reload           apply edited exclusion/policy files
  sudo carlos-ctl waf detect-only      stop blocking, for triage only
  sudo carlos-ctl waf blocking         resume blocking

The audit log is /var/log/carlos-emr/modsec/modsec_audit.log, mode 0640
root:adm inside a 0750 directory — readable by root and group adm, and by
nothing else. Records are JSON carrying headers and matched rules but NOT
request or response bodies (SecAuditLogParts ABFHZ). That is the PHI decision
described under "Log retention", and it is checkable on a running system:
every record has `request.headers` and no `request.body`.

CARLOS is a large application of OSCAR lineage and a clinical workflow you use
may trip a rule the packaged exclusions do not cover. The supported response
is: reproduce it, read `carlos-ctl waf tail` for the rule id and the matched
variable, and add the NARROWEST exclusion that works to

  /etc/carlos-emr/modsecurity/RESPONSE-999-EXCLUSION-RULES-AFTER-CRS.conf

which is a conffile, so your entries survive upgrades. Prefer
`SecRuleUpdateTargetById 942100 "!ARGS:fieldname"` over
`SecRuleRemoveById 942100`: the first unhooks one field from one rule, the
second turns the rule off for the whole application.

Do not leave a system holding patient data in detect-only mode.


6. The database and its schema
------------------------------

Installing this package RESTARTS MariaDB once, if it has to. The settings in
/etc/mysql/mariadb.conf.d/60-carlos-emr.cnf are read only at server start, and
mariadb-server is already running by the time this package is configured — so
without the restart the deployment silently runs on distribution defaults:
STRICT sql_mode (which the OSCAR-lineage application does not expect) and no
binary log, meaning no point-in-time recovery and a nightly backup that fails.
`carlos-ctl db-apply-settings` compares the RUNNING values against the file and
restarts only when they disagree, so an upgrade that changes nothing never
bounces your database.

On Ubuntu 26.04 the MariaDB data directory is /var/lib/mariadb, not
/var/lib/mysql — it moved in 1:11.8.6-5 for MySQL co-installability. The binary
log setting in the drop-in is therefore RELATIVE (log_bin = binlog) so that it
follows the datadir; an absolute /var/lib/mysql/binlog stops the server from
starting on this release.

MariaDB runs on this host, listening on loopback only, with three
least-privilege accounts and no application ever connecting as root:

  carlos    ALL PRIVILEGES on the EMR schema, nothing anywhere else
  drugref   ALL PRIVILEGES on drugref2 only, so a drug lookup service cannot
            read the patient schema
  backup    read-only on the EMR schema, plus the replication rights that let
            it stream binary logs off the server without being able to read
            the datadir. Deliberately no PROCESS: a leak of backup.env must
            not let someone watch clinicians' live SQL.

Administrative access is root@localhost over the unix socket, which on Ubuntu
authenticates by uid. That is why this package never asks for, and never
stores, a MariaDB root password.

The schema is managed with Flyway. The application boots with
`carlos.flyway.onBoot=validate`, so it refuses to start against a schema it was
not built for rather than failing later with a column-not-found error in the
middle of a consultation. Migrations are applied explicitly:

  sudo carlos-ctl db-info              what is applied and what is pending
  sudo carlos-ctl db-validate          does the schema match the deployed WAR
  sudo carlos-ctl db-migrate           apply pending migrations

TAKE A BACKUP YOU HAVE VERIFIED BEFORE MIGRATING. DDL is not transactional in
MariaDB: a migration that fails part way can leave a partially-changed schema.

MariaDB strictness. The drop-in leaves `sql_mode` empty, which is what the
OSCAR-lineage application expects: over-length and out-of-range values are
coerced rather than rejected, and zero dates are accepted. Enabling
STRICT_TRANS_TABLES is a genuine data-integrity improvement and a genuine
application-compatibility risk; validate it on a copy of your data first.

Adopting an existing pre-Flyway (OSCAR 19 / OpenO) database is a two-step
operation and NOT what `db-migrate` does on its own:

  sudo carlos-ctl db-baseline          stamp the history at the full genesis
  sudo carlos-ctl db-migrate           apply only the forward deltas

The baseline is stamped at 1.0.2, not 1. Stamping at 1 would make Flyway re-run
the province migrations, and those DROP and recreate province tables — data
loss.


7. Backups and restore   (DECIDE THIS)
--------------------------------------

The nightly timer produces three encrypted restic snapshots:

  db      a consistent logical dump taken with --single-transaction, recording
          the binary-log coordinates it corresponds to
  binlog  every closed binary log, streamed off the server with the backup
          account's replication right (never read from the datadir, which is
          0700 mysql:mysql). MariaDB's mariadb-binlog has no --raw, so these
          are decoded SQL and replay with "mariadb < file".
  files   patient documents and the configuration

Together those support point-in-time recovery, not just "last night".

  sudo carlos-ctl backup status       when the last backup and drill succeeded
  sudo carlos-ctl backup full         take one now (drives the same systemd
                                      unit as the nightly timer, so manual and
                                      scheduled runs are the same code path)
  sudo carlos-ctl backup verify        restore the newest dump into a scratch
                                      database and check it loaded
  sudo carlos-ctl backup snapshots     list the repository

The weekly restore drill is the part that matters. A backup nobody has restored
is a hypothesis; the drill is what distinguishes "backups ran" from "backups
work".

  ** THE DEFAULT REPOSITORY IS A DIRECTORY ON THIS MACHINE. **
  ** THAT IS NOT DISASTER RECOVERY. **

Set an offsite RESTIC_REPOSITORY in /etc/carlos-emr/backup.env before you
consider the deployment finished, and copy RESTIC_PASSWORD somewhere off this
host. Without that passphrase every backup ever written is permanently
unreadable. The same applies to `encryption.util.secret.key` in
carlos.properties: it decrypts stored third-party credentials, it is inside the
backup, and it must also be escrowed separately.

Set CARLOS_BACKUP_ALERT_WEBHOOK or CARLOS_BACKUP_ALERT_EMAIL. A backup failure
nobody hears about is the same as no backup.

To restore a live system (this stops the EMR, so it needs root). Every restic
invocation goes through the wrapper's restic passthrough — a bare restic would
not load the repository location and passphrase from backup.env. This exact
sequence has been exercised end to end (DROP DATABASE and back):

  sudo systemctl stop carlos-emr
  # Also stop the backup timers for the duration: a nightly or drill run
  # firing mid-restore would wipe its work directory, take a "backup" of the
  # half-loaded database, and fight for the repository lock. (Persistent=true
  # means a missed nightly fires immediately at boot on a rebuilt host —
  # exactly when you are most likely to be doing this.)
  sudo systemctl stop carlos-emr-backup.timer carlos-emr-backup-verify.timer
  # Stopping a timer does NOT stop a run it already started (a nightly can
  # legitimately run for hours, and on a rebuilt host Persistent=true fires
  # the missed run seconds after boot). Stop any in-flight instance too —
  # a no-op if none is running; if one was, expect its "aborted" alert:
  sudo systemctl stop carlos-emr-backup.service carlos-emr-backup-verify.service
  # 0. ON A REBUILT/REPLACEMENT HOST ONLY (skip if you are restoring in place
  #    and the documents + /etc/carlos-emr are intact): restore the FILES
  #    snapshot — the scanned patient documents under OscarDocument AND
  #    /etc/carlos-emr/carlos.properties, which carries encryption.util.secret.key.
  #    This MUST precede the database load: the DB holds third-party credentials
  #    encrypted under that AES key, so a fresh key (which the package generates
  #    on install) would leave them permanently undecryptable. If you escrowed
  #    the key separately (you were told to), putting that copy in place is
  #    equivalent to this step.
  sudo carlos-ctl backup restic restore latest --tag files \
      --target /var/backups/carlos-emr/dr-files
  #    Move the documents into place (owned by the application user):
  sudo rsync -a /var/backups/carlos-emr/dr-files/var/lib/carlos-emr/OscarDocument/ \
      /var/lib/carlos-emr/OscarDocument/
  sudo chown -R carlos:carlos /var/lib/carlos-emr/OscarDocument
  #    Restore the AES key line into the freshly-generated carlos.properties
  #    (copy encryption.util.secret.key from the restored copy, or restore the
  #    whole file if this host has no newer local settings):
  #    (the package writes this line as "key = value", with spaces):
  sudo grep -E '^encryption\.util\.secret\.key[[:space:]]*=' \
      /var/backups/carlos-emr/dr-files/etc/carlos-emr/carlos.properties
  #    ...then set that exact line in /etc/carlos-emr/carlos.properties
  #    (replacing the freshly-generated one) and run:
  #      sudo carlos-ctl init-config
  # 1. The newest dump. The target is OUTSIDE work/ deliberately: work/ is
  #    wiped by every backup and drill run's cleanup, and a restore can
  #    outlive one of those even with the timers stopped (a manual run, a
  #    second operator).
  sudo carlos-ctl backup restic restore latest --tag db \
      --target /var/backups/carlos-emr/dr
  # 2. Re-create the database, then load. The dump deliberately carries no
  #    CREATE DATABASE (it is scoped to the schema's contents), so after a
  #    DROP DATABASE this step fails with "Unknown database" without it —
  #    live-tested:
  sudo mariadb -e "CREATE DATABASE oscar
      CHARACTER SET utf8mb4 COLLATE utf8mb4_general_ci"
  #    SET SESSION sql_log_bin=0 matters: without it the recovery itself is
  #    written into the binary log and becomes part of the very chain the
  #    next backup ships.
  sudo bash -c "mariadb --init-command='SET SESSION sql_log_bin=0' oscar \
      < /var/backups/carlos-emr/dr/.../oscar.sql"
  #    (sudo bash -c, not a bare redirect: YOUR shell opens the file before
  #    sudo elevates, and /var/backups/carlos-emr is 0700 carlos-backup.)
  # 3. Roll forward past the dump, if recovering to later than last night.
  #    The dump records its anchor near its top:
  #      CHANGE MASTER TO MASTER_LOG_FILE='binlog.0000NN', MASTER_LOG_POS=P
  #    The run rotates the binary log immediately before dumping, so the
  #    anchor sits at the START of file NN: the dump holds everything before
  #    the anchor, and file NN onward holds everything after it — including
  #    transactions committed while the dump itself was running.
  #    Each nightly run ships ONE binlog snapshot holding only that run's
  #    newly-closed logs, so the window may span several snapshots — restore
  #    every one taken since the dump, then replay, in order, the files whose
  #    number is GREATER THAN OR EQUAL TO the anchor's NN:
  sudo carlos-ctl backup restic snapshots --tag binlog
  sudo carlos-ctl backup restic restore <each-id> \
      --target /var/backups/carlos-emr/dr
  sudo bash -c "mariadb --init-command='SET SESSION sql_log_bin=0' oscar \
      < /var/backups/carlos-emr/dr/.../binlog.0000MM.sql"  # each MM >= NN
  #    Shipped logs are decoded SQL scoped to the oscar database, so they
  #    replay cleanly onto it. Rare edge: if the anchor position P is larger
  #    than the first event offset in NN's decoded SQL (a transaction slipped
  #    in between the rotation and the dump taking its snapshot), the slice of
  #    NN before the "# at P" line is already inside the dump — you MUST
  #    delete the statements above that line before replaying NN. Do not
  #    replay it untrimmed: the mariadb client ABORTS at the first
  #    duplicate-key error (there is no --force here), so everything in NN
  #    after that error — the transactions you are trying to recover — would
  #    silently never be applied.
  #    RECOVERING TO A POINT, not to now (you are restoring because
  #    something BAD was written — replaying everything would faithfully
  #    replay the bad change too): find the first event past your target in
  #    the decoded SQL (each event group is headed by a
  #    "#YYMMDD HH:MM:SS server id ..." line), DELETE from that line to the
  #    end of that file, replay the truncated file LAST, and do not replay
  #    any later file. Those header timestamps are in the timezone the
  #    SERVER host was running when the log was shipped (its system zone,
  #    often UTC on cloud images) — NOT necessarily the clinic's CARLOS_TZ;
  #    convert your target time before cutting.
  #    One caveat on the shipped logs themselves: they are decoded with
  #    --database=oscar, which keeps every row change but can only keep
  #    statement-logged events (DDL such as ALTER/TRUNCATE) when the issuing
  #    session had oscar as its default database. carlos-ctl db-migrate does;
  #    run any manual DDL as `mariadb oscar` (not cross-schema) or it will be
  #    absent from the replay.
  sudo carlos-ctl db-validate
  # If drugref2 is also gone (a decommissioned host, a fresh rebuild): it has
  # no backup leg BY DESIGN — the dataset rebuilds from the packaged seed:
  #   sudo mariadb -e 'DROP DATABASE IF EXISTS drugref2'   # only if half-present
  #   sudo dpkg-reconfigure carlos-emr-drugref
  # (Live-tested: until you do, drug lookups fail and 'carlos-ctl check'
  # reports DrugRef not answering.)
  # The restored dump and decoded logs are plaintext PHI — remove them
  # (and the files-restore staging, if you used step 0).
  sudo rm -rf /var/backups/carlos-emr/dr /var/backups/carlos-emr/dr-files
  sudo systemctl start carlos-emr-backup.timer carlos-emr-backup-verify.timer
  sudo systemctl start carlos-emr


8. Upgrades
-----------

`apt upgrade` of this package stops the application FIRST (the packaging's
preinst stops the service and timers before unpack — replacing the exploded
webapp underneath a running JVM is not safe), replaces the webapp, re-renders
the derived configuration, runs `carlos-ctl db-migrate`, and starts the
service again. Plan the maintenance window for the whole operation: unpack of
the ~400 MB webapp plus migration plus the ~2-minute redeploy.

Your conffiles are offered as three-way merges. Your credential files are left
alone. Read /usr/share/carlos-emr/skel/ afterwards to see whether new settings
are available that your files predate.

If a migration fails, the service will not start (the boot-time validate gate
is doing its job). Fix the cause, run `carlos-ctl db-migrate`, then
`systemctl start carlos-emr`.


9. Log retention   (DECIDE THIS)
--------------------------------

/etc/logrotate.d/carlos-emr keeps 30 days. These logs are PHI-adjacent:

  - the Tomcat access log records which authenticated user reached which
    application route, and from which client address. It deliberately logs the
    path but NOT the query string, because query strings on this application
    carry PHI-correlating identifiers.
  - the ModSecurity audit log (/var/log/carlos-emr/modsec/) records the
    request line, headers and matched rule for blocked requests. It is
    configured with SecAuditLogParts ABFHZ, which excludes request and
    response BODIES — those are where clinical free text and health-card
    numbers would land on every false positive.

Only the ModSecurity logs are handled by logrotate. Tomcat's access log, the
JULI container log and the application's own Log4j2 files each rotate and
expire themselves (30, 30 and 7 generations respectively); putting logrotate
on top of a file its writer already manages is how audit trails end up
truncated. The relevant settings are in /etc/carlos-emr/tomcat/server.xml,
/etc/carlos-emr/tomcat/logging.properties, and the application's log4j2.xml.

All of them are included in the encrypted backup. Match the retention here,
and in backup.env, to your own obligations.


10. What this package deliberately does not do
----------------------------------------------

  - It does not configure a host firewall. Ubuntu ships ufw and nftables;
    which one a site uses, and what else the machine serves, is not something
    a package should decide. Allow 22, 80 and 443 and deny the rest.
  - It does not set up an observability stack. Use the journal
    (`carlos-ctl logs`), or the carlos-podman deployment if you want metrics
    and log search.
  - It does not run the EMR on more than one host, and must not be used that
    way: several nodes against one database would each run the boot-time
    schema gate independently.
  - It does not manage clinical configuration — labs, faxes, HRM, billing
    submission. Those are settings inside the EMR.
  - It does not encrypt the disk. On a machine holding patient records, use
    full-disk encryption.


11. Troubleshooting
-------------------

  sudo carlos-ctl check                start here; it names the failure
  sudo carlos-ctl logs -n 200          application server log
  sudo journalctl -u nginx -n 100     front door
  sudo tail -f /var/log/carlos-emr/modsec/modsec_audit.log

The application will not start
  Almost always the schema gate or the database. `carlos-ctl db-info` shows the
  migration state; `systemctl status mariadb` shows the database.

A page returns 403 and the application log shows nothing
  That is the WAF, not the application. `carlos-ctl waf tail` shows why.

Login redirects to http:// or the session is lost
  Tomcat's RemoteIpValve has stopped trusting nginx, so the application no
  longer sees X-Forwarded-Proto. Check the `internalProxies` pattern in
  /etc/carlos-emr/tomcat/server.xml and the proxy_set_header lines in
  /etc/carlos-emr/nginx/proxy-params.conf.

Drug lookups return nothing when prescribing
  Install carlos-emr-drugref, or check that it deployed:
  `carlos-ctl logs | grep drugref2`.


12. The other deployment: carlos-podman
---------------------------------------

A container-based CARLOS deployment is under development at

  https://github.com/carlos-emr/carlos-podman

It runs CARLOS as rootless Podman pods — a separate WAF pod, an optional
observability stack (metrics, log search, alerting), TPM-sealed secrets, and
Ansible-driven multi-instance provisioning. It suits sites that want that
richer operational surface, where this package deliberately stays simple.
Its own documentation lives in the repository's README and QUICKSTART:

  https://github.com/carlos-emr/carlos-podman#readme
  https://github.com/carlos-emr/carlos-podman/blob/main/QUICKSTART.md

The two deployments share the `carlos-ctl` administration name and the
overlapping verb set on purpose, so what you learned here transfers.
